  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Three objects <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>B</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math> are kept in a straight line on a frictionless horizontal surface. The masses of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>B</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></math> respectively. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> moves towards <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> with a speed of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> and makes an elastic collision with it. Thereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> makes a completely inelastic collision with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi><mo>.</mo></math> All motions occur along the same straight line. The final speed of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math> is :</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0050_2a9965177ef4.png\" style=\"width: 280px; height: 82px;\" /></p>"
  },
